 the law so the idea of closing  the loophole is something that we're  fighting at the state level where we  have a Republican Senate that refuses  I'm sorry a Republican Republicans in  the Senate that are refusing to support  the this you know closing of the  loophole so there's work that we need to  do with our allies and and with all of  you in terms of really making the  message and taking the message to  legislators but I really want to commend  the work that many of you are doing and  for this film I think it really brings  it home and and for myself that  represents a constituency that deals  with this issue each and every day I  just see how unjust it is how  ineffective it is and that we have a  long way to go but that we need to  continue to fight the good fight  I'm John Tierney and I want a really  Paul and David and for the film it's  just it is really heartwarming to see it  I've been writing about this for a long  time and I I know some of the people in  the movie and Ethan and Radley Balko and  you know they've just been doing amazing  work and it's so frustrating when you  cover this for a while and see how great  the arguments are how terrible the  stories are and things don't change now  watching the story about Robert Moss  reminded me last year for the New York  Times I did a series about incarceration  and I did one article about people  serving mandatory life sentences with no  parole for drugs and there were just  awful stories that people were  nonviolent they you know some of them  had been you know there was a Grateful  Dead head who was caught with some LSD  that they trumped up the weight they  counted the paper that the blotter was  on and sent him to prison for life  there's a woman who's whose boyfriend  it's towards some some drugs in her  house without her knowledge and you know  he got out in a few years as did  everyone else but she's serving life  without parole and there were about five  stories like this I wrote about and and 