blicans love to moralize about it  Democrats you know they're not so moral  about this and Obama of course is not is  himself a user but they stay I've  wondered why don't liberals take this  more seriously and I sometimes think  it's really because they may like drugs  or they may hate drug laws but they love  big government even more and you see  these very unholy alliances and  Republicans and Democrats with prison  guard unions with police forces that are  all profiting from this war and it's  simply nobody really wants to stand up  and take the money away  what I find hopeful though is seeing a  movie like this seeing what reason does  for this and seeing you know the army of  David as Glenn Reynolds calls it that  you know that the the sentiment of the  public sentiment against drugs is very  strong but it's never really had a voice  because the establishment at both the  local and the federal level you know  there's a lot of money in it for people  there but you know people who see this  effect on their families and their  communities are not in favor of it it  was striking I think when the White  House  had a website where they were asking  people what question they asked the  president that the marijuana question  would just come to the top all the time  of course nobody in washington the  washington press corps is really too  sophisticated to care about that anymore  it's you know they know that's this kind  of a  it's sort of one of these strange  libertarian issues but people really do  care about it and I think with the rise  of the Tea Party with Rand Paul see you  know coming out against now against  mandatory minimums I think there's a  chance that there can be you know that  the grassroots can be heard on this and  I hope Rand Paul will make this an issue  I'm not sure you know he's how strong he  is on drugs I know he's trying to he's  trying to play the conservative base but  I think that you know the more we have  the web thanks to the web we have more  of a chance for people to actually rally  around this issue and to make some  headway against these established forces  that are doing it and finally they you  know there is some hope I think at the  state level that they simply can't  afford to in to incarcerate all these  people anymore and so you are seeing on  both sides of the aisle on you and some  Republican governors have led the way in  this realizing we just can't afford to  keep building prisons to lock these  people up so I'm hoping that between  popular organization this army of David  the that has a way to reach each other  in between fiscal realities that  something can happen but but in the  meantime I think this movie is just an  amazing achievement it's just it's  maddening that's heartwarming at the  same time and congratulations  yeah just second that really excellent  film a congratulations to you and David  not a reason foundation for putting the  resources behind us and putting this out  there  I think what might be most helpful cuz  you already heard me up there you know  the crying how terrible this drug war is  how long it's been going on America's  massive incarceration policy the  incredibly destructive role around the  world not just in Mexico and the  Caribbean Latin America but now in West  Africa and a whole range of other parts  world so enough with that I mean enough  cuz you heard it but I thought what  might be most helpful is just you know  things are moving in a more positive  direction on drug policy reform and  criminal justice reform right now than  at any time in all the years I've been  involved in this and you know it's still  this massive you know ocean liner of a  drug war prison industrial complex  moving forward and with all this  momentum behind it but things are  changing and I just want to pick out a  few things you heard me say Obama was  such a terrible disappointment the  second half of his first term but I'll  say this those guys kept telling us this  is